A database-diff engine that compares schemas and generates migration scripts.
Point it at a source and a target database, and it emits the DDL needed to make
the target match the source. The core is database-agnostic, so SQL Server,
PostgreSQL and MySQL are all in scope.

# Approach

The architecture keeps core logic completely separate from database-specific
mechanisms, and works through three models:

- Introspection & Canonical Schema: adapters read a database's metadata and
convert it into a canonical schema model — the data as it actually is, in an
abstract, DB-agnostic form.
- Diff Engine & Migration Plan: the engine reads two canonical schemas and
computes a migration plan, an intermediate representation of explicit,
directional operations (`AddColumnOperation`, `CreateTableOperation`, ...)
that turn the target into the source.
- Renderer & SQL Generation: a target-specific renderer takes the plan and
generates native database logic, like T-SQL DDL scripts.

Adding another engine mainly means writing a new Introspector and Renderer —
the diffing engine itself stays untouched. The v1 foundation ships the core
models, the CLI, and a skeletal MSSQL adapter, with data operations planned
alongside schema operations in later phases.
